+// Per SENDER, how many frames ARRIVED at each key index in the last window and
+// how many of those never reached the decoder. Flushed to the main thread once
+// a second.
+//
+// Why it lives HERE and not in `RTCRtpReceiver.getStats()`. The chip's green was
+// the DEFAULT: it went amber only while a verdict OBJECT existed, so a verdict
+// destroyed without evidence -- or never created -- read as green over frames
+// this worker was silently dropping. A getStats witness cannot fix that:
+// `framesDecoded` and `totalSamplesReceived - concealedSamples` are counted
+// AFTER the SIF passthrough, so SFU-injected blank/silence frames advance them
+// for a participant whose real frames are being discarded.
+//
+// `dropped` counts NOT ENQUEUED, measured at the exit of `decodeFunction`.
+// The first cut counted one branch -- `hasInvalidKeyAtIndex` -- and there are
+// five ways out of that function that discard a frame: the SIF-reject arm, a
+// missing key set, a decrypt that throws a CryptorError, a decrypt that throws
+// anything ELSE (an AV1 payload makes `getUnencryptedBytes` throw a plain
+// Error, which marks no index invalid and emits nothing), and a decrypt that
+// resolves falsy. Four of the five were being tallied as healthy traffic.
+//
+// 🔴 `keyIndex` is the frame's last byte, which is the key index ONLY for a
+// frame that reached the cryptor. For a SIF-rejected frame those bytes are
+// trailer, and for an empty DTX frame there is none (-1). The DROP is exact on
+// every path; the INDEX is advisory, so nothing may derive a per-index verdict
+// from this tally without re-establishing the index another way.
